\title{\bfseries The Structural Orthogonality Framework:\\
Eight Barriers to Classical Integer Factoring}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{12 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
We present a unified framework of eight structural barriers that explains the
uniform failure of a large class of proposed classical approaches to integer
factoring. The framework was synthesized from a systematic experimental
programme in which $284$ candidate invariants drawn from more than sixty
paradigms were tested against a common protocol --- the \emph{near-equal-$N$
test} --- and every one of them measured as uninformative about the hidden
factors.

Three barriers are rigidity theorems. The \emph{polynomial barrier} states that
no rational function, and more generally no nonzero algebraic relation over
$\QQ$, connects a semiprime $N=pq$ to its smaller prime factor $p$; a
quantitative refinement bounds the number of semiprimes below $X$ on which a
fixed degree-$d$ polynomial succeeds by $d\,\pi(\sqrt X)\le d(\sqrt X+1)$. The
\emph{symmetry barrier} states that every power-sum invariant of the factor pair
is a fixed polynomial in the elementary symmetric functions $s=p+q$ and $N=pq$,
so factor pairs sharing $(N,s)$ are indistinguishable by the whole family at
once. The \emph{meromorphic rigidity barrier} states that no function
meromorphic at the origin satisfies $f(1/N)=1/p$ across the semiprimes.

Five further barriers are structural patterns made precise: \emph{computational
circularity} ($(N,s)$ factors $N$ in closed form, and $\sigma(N)$, $\varphi(N)$
both reveal $s$); \emph{known-method-in-disguise} (for odd $N$, a nontrivial
difference-of-squares representation is equivalent to a nontrivial
factorization); the \emph{multiplicative dichotomy} in arbitrary degree; and,
at the centre, \emph{free-witness aggregation} and \emph{structural
orthogonality}: every invariant $g\circ n$ computable from the band label alone
is exactly orthogonal to the residual $Y-E[Y\mid n]$. From this single identity
follow the covariance identity, the near-equal-$N$ test, optimality of the band
mean among all $N$-only predictors, the band-spread law, and extensions to
adaptive decision trees, randomized mixtures, and finite-palette strategies,
where a depth--advantage collapse holds. We also delimit the framework: the
smaller prime factor \emph{is} a function of $N$ alone, so the barriers are
structural rather than information-theoretic.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} integer factoring; semiprimes; structural
orthogonality; conditional expectation; band-spread law; polynomial rigidity;
meromorphic rigidity; multiplicative dichotomy; adaptive lower bounds.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The problem and the persistence of the search}

Let $p<q$ be distinct primes and $N=pq$ their product, a \emph{semiprime}.
Recovering $p$ from $N$ is the integer factoring problem in its
cryptographically relevant form. The best known classical algorithm, the general
number field sieve, runs in time
\[
L_N\!\left[\tfrac13,\sqrt[3]{64/9}\right]
=\exp\!\Bigl((1.9229\ldots+o(1))(\log N)^{1/3}(\log\log N)^{2/3}\Bigr),
\]
sub-exponential but super-polynomial; the only known algorithm running in time
polynomial in $\log N$ requires a quantum computer.

The gap between these regimes has attracted a correspondingly large volume of
proposals: closed-form expressions, continued fractions, analytic continuations,
spectral heuristics, statistical ``witnesses'', and learned predictors trained
on features of $N$. They fail. The interesting question is not \emph{that} they
fail but \emph{whether they fail for a common reason}.

\subsection{The experimental programme}

The framework was distilled from a systematic programme: $284$ candidate
invariants, gathered from more than sixty paradigms, each subjected to the same
protocol.

\begin{quote}
\textbf{The near-equal-$N$ test.} Group semiprimes into narrow size bands (in
practice by $\lfloor N/40\rfloor$). Within a band the modulus is essentially
constant while the hidden factor pair varies widely. Compute the candidate
invariant across the band and measure its correlation with the smaller factor
$p$. An invariant that varies across the band but correlates $\approx 0$ with
$p$ after controlling for $N$ is declared \emph{$N$-only}.
\end{quote}

Every invariant computable from $N$ alone tested as $N$-only. This paper
explains why.

\subsection{Overview}

