\# & Barrier & Content \\
\midrule
1 & Polynomial / algebraic & No nonzero algebraic relation over $\QQ$ links $N$ and $p$\\
2 & Counting & A degree-$d$ formula succeeds on $O(d\sqrt X)$ semiprimes below $X$\\
3 & Meromorphic rigidity & No function meromorphic at $0$ realizes $f(1/N)=1/p$\\
4 & Symmetry & Power-sum invariants are functions of $(s,N)$ only\\
5 & Computational circularity & $(N,s)$ factors $N$ in closed form; $\sigma$, $\varphi$ reveal $s$\\
6 & Known-method-in-disguise & Difference of squares $\equiv$ Fermat factorization\\
7 & Multiplicative dichotomy & Every polynomial multiplicative invariant is constant-on-fibres or sum-revealing\\
8 & Structural orthogonality & Every $N$-only statistic is orthogonal to $Y-E[Y\mid n]$\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Barrier 8 subsumes free-witness aggregation, adaptivity, randomization and
quantization as corollaries.

\subsection{What is not claimed}

This is a research deliverable, not a factoring breakthrough, and not a proof
that factoring is hard. Two facts, proved in Section~\ref{sec:boundary},
constrain everything: the smaller prime factor \emph{is} a function of $N$
alone, so no barrier here can be information-theoretic; and an $N$-only
invariant \emph{can} correlate with $p$ across bands, so every zero-correlation
statement must be conditional on the band structure.

\section{Notation}

Throughout, $p<q$ are distinct primes and $N=pq$; we write $s=p+q$ for the
factor sum. For the probabilistic part, $\Omega$ is a finite nonempty
population, $n:\Omega\to\kappa$ the band map, and $Y:\Omega\to\RR$ the target
(in the application $Y(i)$ is the smaller factor of the $i$-th semiprime). All
expectations are with respect to the uniform law:
\[
E[Z]=\frac{1}{|\Omega|}\sum_{i\in\Omega}Z(i),\qquad
\Cov(X,Y)=E[XY]-E[X]E[Y],\qquad \Var(X)=\Cov(X,X).
\]
The \emph{band} of $i$ is $\Band(i)=\{j\in\Omega: n(j)=n(i)\}$ and the
\emph{band mean} is
\[
E[Y\mid n](i)=\frac{1}{|\Band(i)|}\sum_{j\in\Band(i)}Y(j).
\]
An \emph{$N$-only invariant} is a random variable of the form $i\mapsto g(n(i))$.

\section{Rigidity barriers: algebra}

\subsection{The polynomial and rational barriers}

\begin{theorem}[Polynomial barrier]\label{thm:poly}
There is no $P\in\QQ[X]$ with $P(pq)=p$ for all primes $p<q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Rational escape is illusory]\label{thm:rat}
There are no $A,B\in\QQ[X]$ such that for all primes $p<q$ one has
$B(pq)\ne 0$ and $A(pq)=p\,B(pq)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Theorem~\ref{thm:poly} is the case $B=1$. For Theorem~\ref{thm:rat}, set
$C=A-3B$. For $p=3$ and any prime $q>3$ the hypothesis gives $C(3q)=0$. Since
there are infinitely many primes $q>3$ and $q\mapsto 3q$ is injective, $C$ has
infinitely many roots, so $C=0$ and $A=3B$ identically. Evaluating at
$N=35=5\cdot 7$ gives $5B(35)=A(35)=3B(35)$, whence $B(35)=0$, contradicting
non-vanishing.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
All three algebraic barriers share one mechanism: \emph{fixing the small factor
makes the sample points accumulate}, rigidity of the function class forces a
constant, and a second choice of small factor contradicts it.
\end{remark}

\subsection{The algebraic barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Algebraic barrier]\label{thm:alg}
Let $F$ be a polynomial in two variables over $\QQ$, written as
$F\in(\QQ[X])[Y]$. If $F(N,p)=0$ for every semiprime $N=pq$ with $p<q$ prime,
then $F=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Two applications of ``a nonzero polynomial over an integral domain has finitely
many roots''. First fix a prime $p$: the specialization $H_p(X)=F(X,p)\in\QQ[X]$
vanishes at $pq$ for every prime $q>p$, infinitely many distinct rationals, so
$H_p=0$. Second, regard $F$ as a polynomial in $Y$ over the domain $\QQ[X]$; the
constants $p$, one per prime, are infinitely many distinct roots, so $F=0$.
\end{proof}

Theorems~\ref{thm:poly} and~\ref{thm:rat} are corollaries: the relations
$Y-P(X)$ and $A(X)-YB(X)$ would be nonzero witnesses.

\subsection{The counting barrier}

\begin{theorem}[Per-factor counting bound]\label{thm:count1}
Let $P\in\QQ[X]$ and $p$ a prime with $P\ne p$ as polynomials. Then
$\#\{q\text{ prime}: q>p,\ P(pq)=p\}\le\deg P$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each such $q$ makes $pq$ a root of the nonzero polynomial $P-p$, and $q\mapsto
pq$ is injective.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Global counting barrier]\label{thm:count2}
Let $P\in\QQ[X]$ with $\deg P\ge 1$ and $X\ge 1$. Then
\[
\#\{(p,q): p<q\text{ prime},\ pq\le X,\ P(pq)=p\}
\le \deg P\cdot\pi(\sqrt X)\le \deg P\,(\sqrt X+1).
\]
The analogous bound holds for rational functions $A/B$ with $\deg P$ replaced by
$\max(\deg A,\deg B)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition the success set by the small factor. Since $p<q$ and $pq\le X$ we have
$p\le\sqrt X$, so only $\pi(\sqrt X)$ values of $p$ occur, and each fibre has at
most $\deg P$ elements by Theorem~\ref{thm:count1}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The number of semiprimes below $X$ is asymptotically $X\log\log X/\log X$, so
the success density of any fixed polynomial or rational formula is
$O(X^{-1/2}\log X/\log\log X)\to 0$. As soon as the number of semiprimes below
$X$ exceeds $\deg P\,(\sqrt X+1)$, an explicit failure exists below $X$.
\end{corollary}

\section{Rigidity barriers: analysis}

Rather than asking for $f(N)=p$, we invert and sample at $1/N$: the reciprocals
of semiprimes with a fixed small factor accumulate at the origin, where rigidity
bites.

\begin{theorem}[Holomorphic rigidity barrier]\label{thm:hrb}
There is no entire $f:\CC\to\CC$ with $f(1/(pq))=1/p$ for all primes $p<q$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Fix $p=3$. The points $u_k=1/(3q_k)$, with $q_k$ the increasing primes above
$3$, are nonzero and tend to $0$, and $f(u_k)=1/3$ for all $k$. By the identity
theorem applied to $f-1/3$, $f\equiv 1/3$. But $f(1/35)=1/5$, and $1/3\ne 1/5$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Meromorphic rigidity barrier]\label{thm:mrb}
The same conclusion holds under the far weaker hypothesis that $f$ is merely
meromorphic at $0$, i.e.\ has an isolated non-essential singularity there. In
particular no entire function, no finite-order entire function, no rational
function and no function with a finite pole at the origin satisfies
$f(1/N)=1/p$ across the semiprimes. Only the two families $p=3$ and $p=5$ are
used, so the barrier applies to functions asked to compute the factor only for
these two small primes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Replace the identity theorem by the local dichotomy for isolated singularities:
a function meromorphic at $0$ either vanishes on a punctured neighbourhood of
$0$ or is nonzero on one. Apply it to $g=f-1/3$. The points $1/(3q)$ accumulate
at $0$ and $g$ vanishes there, ruling out the second alternative; hence $g$
vanishes identically near $0$. But the points $1/(5q)$ also accumulate at $0$
and there $g=1/5-1/3\ne0$.
\end{proof}

\section{Symmetry and circularity}

\subsection{The symmetry barrier}

\begin{definition}
For $e_1,e_2\in\ZZ$ define the \emph{Newton sequence}
$T_0=2$, $T_1=e_1$, $T_{k+2}=e_1T_{k+1}-e_2T_k$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Newton's identity; symmetry barrier]\label{thm:sym}
For all integers $p,q$ and all $k\ge0$, $T_k(p+q,pq)=p^k+q^k$. Consequently, if
$pq=p'q'$ and $p+q=p'+q'$, then $p^k+q^k=p'^k+q'^k$ for every $k$: the two
factorizations are indistinguishable by the entire family of power-sum
invariants simultaneously.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Strong induction on $k$ using
$p^{k+2}+q^{k+2}=(p+q)(p^{k+1}+q^{k+1})-pq(p^k+q^k)$. The content is the
\emph{shape}: the recursion refers only to $(e_1,e_2)$, never to $p$ and $q$
separately.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Computational circularity}

\begin{theorem}[Closed-form recovery from the sum]\label{thm:recovery}
Let $p\le q$ be integers, $N=pq$, $s=p+q$. Then $s^2-4N=(q-p)^2$ is a perfect
square and
\[
p=\frac{s-\sqrt{s^2-4N}}{2},\qquad q=\frac{s+\sqrt{s^2-4N}}{2}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Circularity of $\sigma$ and $\varphi$]\label{thm:circ}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and $N=pq$,
\[
\sigma(N)=(1+p)(1+q)=N+1+s,\qquad \varphi(N)=(p-1)(q-1)=N+1-s.
\]
Hence $s=\sigma(N)-N-1=N+1-\varphi(N)$, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:recovery} either
invariant determines the factorization in closed form. Computing $\sigma$ or
$\varphi$ for a semiprime is therefore at least as hard as factoring it.
\end{theorem}

Theorems~\ref{thm:sym}--\ref{thm:circ} form the \emph{circularity trap}: an
invariant respecting the symmetry of the factor pair is blind, while one
breaking it is a factoring algorithm.

\subsection{Known-method-in-disguise}

\begin{theorem}[Fermat equivalence]\label{thm:fermat}
Let $N$ be odd. Then there exist $a,b\in\ZZ$ with $0\le b$, $b+1<a$ and
$N=a^2-b^2$ if and only if there exist $u,v\in\ZZ$ with $1<u\le v$ and $N=uv$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$(\Rightarrow)$ Take $u=a-b$, $v=a+b$; $b+1<a$ gives $u>1$. $(\Leftarrow)$ $N$
odd forces $u=2m+1$, $v=2n+1$ odd; then $a=m+n+1$, $b=n-m$ satisfy
$a^2-b^2=uv=N$ with $b\ge 0$ and $a>b+1$.
\end{proof}

\section{The multiplicative dichotomy in arbitrary degree}

\subsection{The classical invariants}

\begin{theorem}[Constant side]\label{thm:const}
For distinct primes $p,q$ and $N=pq$: $\tau(N)=4$, $\omega(N)=2$, $\mu(N)=1$.
These are literally constant on the semiprimes and cannot distinguish any two of
them, let alone their factors.
\end{theorem}

Together with Theorem~\ref{thm:circ} this establishes the ``$N$-only or
circular'' pattern for the classical multiplicative invariants: no third
behaviour occurs.

\subsection{Symmetric reduction}

\begin{theorem}[Symmetric reduction identity]\label{thm:symred}
Let $F\in\ZZ[X]$ and $p,q\in\ZZ$. Divide $F$ by the monic quadratic
$(X-p)(X-q)=X^2-sX+N$ and write the remainder as $BX+A$. Then
\[
F(p)=Bp+A,\qquad F(q)=Bq+A,\qquad F(p)F(q)=A^2+ABs+B^2N.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Evaluate $F=Q\cdot(X-p)(X-q)+BX+A$ at $X=p$ and $X=q$, then multiply and use
$p+q=s$, $pq=N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Reduction slope]\label{thm:slope}
If $p\ne q$ then $B=0$ if and only if $F(p)=F(q)$; in that case $F(p)F(q)=A^2$
is a perfect square. Otherwise, if $AB\ne0$, the sum is recovered by a single
division, $s=(T-A^2-B^2N)/(AB)$ where $T=F(p)F(q)$.
\end{theorem}

Here $A$ and $B$ depend on the unknown $p,q$. The next result removes this
objection by performing the reduction \emph{generically}.

\subsection{The universal invariant polynomial}

Work in $\ZZ[N][s]$ with $N$ and $s$ independent formal symbols and reduce
powers of $X$ modulo $X^2-sX+N$ by
\[
X^{k}\equiv B_kX+A_k,\qquad A_{k+1}=-N B_k,\qquad B_{k+1}=B_k s+A_k,
\qquad A_0=1,\ B_0=0 .
\]
Extending linearly to $F=\sum_k c_kX^k$ gives $A_F=\sum_k c_kA_k$,
$B_F=\sum_kc_kB_k$, and we define the \emph{universal invariant polynomial}
\[
\Psi_F(s,N)\;=\;A_F^2+A_FB_F\,s+B_F^2N .
\]

\begin{theorem}[Genericity]\label{thm:generic}
For all integers $p,q$ one has $\Psi_F(p+q,\,pq)=F(p)F(q)$, and
$\deg_s\Psi_F\le 2\deg F$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Apply the evaluation homomorphism $\ZZ[N][s]\to\ZZ$, $s\mapsto p+q$,
$N\mapsto pq$. Under it the generic recursion specializes to the concrete
reduction of Theorem~\ref{thm:symred}, because $r=p$ (or $r=q$) satisfies
$r^2=(p+q)r-pq$. The degree bound follows by induction from
$\deg_sA_F\le\deg F$ and $\deg_sB_F<\deg F$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The dichotomy}

Fix $N$ and set $\psi=\Psi_F(\cdot,N)\in\ZZ[s]$, computable from $N$ and $F$
alone.

\begin{theorem}[General-degree multiplicative dichotomy]\label{thm:dich}
Exactly one of the following holds.
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*)]
\item \emph{($N$-only side.)} $\psi$ is constant; then for every factorization
$N=pq$ one has $F(p)F(q)=\psi(0)$, so the invariant takes one and the same value
on all factor pairs of $N$.
\item \emph{(Circular side.)} $\psi$ is nonconstant of degree at most
$2\deg F$; then for every factorization $N=pq$, writing $T=F(p)F(q)$, the
polynomial $\psi-T$ is nonzero, has $s=p+q$ among its roots, and has at most
$2\deg F$ roots. The hidden sum is therefore one of at most $2\deg F$
explicitly computable candidates, each of which yields a candidate
factorization in closed form by Theorem~\ref{thm:recovery}.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
If $\deg\psi=0$, evaluate Theorem~\ref{thm:generic} at any factorization. If
$\deg\psi\ge1$, subtracting a constant cannot lower the degree, so $\psi-T\ne0$;
Theorem~\ref{thm:generic} gives $(\psi-T)(p+q)=0$; a nonzero polynomial has at
most $\deg$ many roots.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Both branches occur]
For $F=X$ one has $\Psi_X(s,N)=N$, constant: the identity invariant is $N$-only,
as it must be. For $F=X+c$ with $c\ne0$, $\Psi_F(s,N)=N+cs+c^2$ has degree $1$
and $s=(T-N-c^2)/c$. For $F=X^2+1$ and $N=15$ the specialized polynomial takes
the value $260$ at $s=8$, matching $F(3)F(5)=10\cdot26=260$.
\end{corollary}

Thus the empirical trichotomy (``useless / partially informative / equivalent to
factoring'') is in fact a \emph{dichotomy}.

\section{Structural orthogonality: the statistical core}

\subsection{The fundamental identity}

\begin{theorem}[Structural orthogonality]\label{thm:so}
Let $\Omega$ be a finite population, $n:\Omega\to\kappa$ a band map,
$Y:\Omega\to\RR$ a target, and $g:\kappa\to\RR$ arbitrary. Then
\[
\sum_{i\in\Omega} g(n(i))\bigl(Y(i)-E[Y\mid n](i)\bigr)=0 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Partition $\Omega$ into the fibres of $n$ and sum band by band. On a fibre,
$g(n(i))$ is constant and factors out; the remaining sum of residuals vanishes
because the band mean is the average of $Y$ over that band.
\end{proof}

Taking $g\equiv1$ gives the tower property $E\bigl[E[Y\mid n]\bigr]=E[Y]$.

\subsection{Covariance and the near-equal-$N$ test}

\begin{theorem}[All correlation is band correlation]\label{thm:covband}
For every $g$, $\Cov(g\circ n,Y)=\Cov\bigl(g\circ n,E[Y\mid n]\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Near-equal-$N$ test]\label{thm:neqn}
If $E[Y\mid n](i)=c$ for all $i\in\Omega$, then for every $g$,
$\Cov(g\circ n,Y)=0$ and $\Corr(g\circ n,Y)=0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Theorem~\ref{thm:covband} the covariance equals that of $g\circ n$ with a
constant, which vanishes; the correlation is the covariance divided by a product
of standard deviations.
\end{proof}

Grouping semiprimes into narrow bands is precisely an attempt to make
$E[p\mid N\text{-band}]$ constant; to the extent it succeeds, \emph{every}
$N$-only invariant measures zero correlation with $p$ identically, not by
accident.

\subsection{The band mean is the best $N$-only predictor}

\begin{theorem}[Squared-error decomposition]\label{thm:sqerr}
For every $g$,
\[
\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(g(n(i))-Y(i)\bigr)^2
=\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(g(n(i))-E[Y\mid n](i)\bigr)^2
+\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(E[Y\mid n](i)-Y(i)\bigr)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Expand around $E[Y\mid n]$; the cross term vanishes by Theorem~\ref{thm:so}
applied to the band-label function $k\mapsto g(k)-\bar Y_k$, where $\bar Y_k$ is
the band mean as a function of the label.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Best-predictor barrier]\label{cor:best}
For every $g$,
$\sum_i(E[Y\mid n](i)-Y(i))^2\le\sum_i(g(n(i))-Y(i))^2$. No $N$-only invariant
predicts the hidden factor better than the band mean, and the excess error is
exactly the squared deviation from it.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Free-witness aggregation}

\begin{theorem}[Free-witness aggregation]\label{thm:agg}
Let $g_1,\dots,g_m:\kappa\to\RR$ be $N$-only witnesses and
$\Phi:\RR^m\to\RR$ an arbitrary, possibly highly nonlinear, aggregation rule.
Then $i\mapsto\Phi(g_1(n(i)),\dots,g_m(n(i)))$ is itself an $N$-only invariant;
under the constant-band-mean hypothesis its covariance with $Y$ is exactly zero,
and in general it cannot beat the band mean in squared error.
\end{theorem}

This is the barrier that covers learned predictors: any model whose inputs are
functions of $N$ is, whatever its architecture, a function of $N$.

\subsection{The band-spread law}

\begin{theorem}[Cauchy--Schwarz form]\label{thm:cs}
For every $g$, $\Cov(g\circ n,Y)^2\le\Var(g\circ n)\cdot\Var\bigl(E[Y\mid n]\bigr)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Law of total variance]\label{thm:tv}
\[
\Var(Y)=\frac{1}{|\Omega|}\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(Y(i)-E[Y\mid n](i)\bigr)^2
+\Var\bigl(E[Y\mid n]\bigr).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Band-spread law]\label{thm:bsl}
Assume $\Var(Y)>0$. For every $g$,
\[
\bigl|\Corr(g\circ n,Y)\bigr|\le\sqrt{\frac{\Var(E[Y\mid n])}{\Var(Y)}}
=\sqrt{1-\frac{\text{within-band error}}{\Var(Y)}} .
\]
In particular, if $\Var(E[Y\mid n])\le\varepsilon\Var(Y)$ then
$|\Corr(g\circ n,Y)|\le\sqrt\varepsilon$ for every $N$-only invariant; and zero
band spread forces exactly zero correlation.
\end{theorem}

The entire experimental programme therefore reduces to a single scalar quantity
of the population, $\Var(E[p\mid N\text{-band}])$, which involves no invariant at
all.

\section{Adaptivity, randomness, and quantization}

\subsection{Adaptive strategies}

\begin{definition}
A \emph{decision tree} over $\Omega$ is either a leaf carrying a function
$\Omega\to\RR$, or an internal node carrying a predicate on $\Omega$ together
with two subtrees; its evaluation $t(i)$ follows the selected branch. A function
$f$ is \emph{band-measurable} if $n(i)=n(j)$ implies $f(i)=f(j)$ for
$i,j\in\Omega$. A tree is \emph{$N$-only} if every internal predicate and every
leaf function is band-measurable.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:bm}
A band-measurable function factors as $g\circ n$; and the evaluation of an
$N$-only decision tree is band-measurable.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Adaptive barrier]\label{thm:adapt}
For every $N$-only decision tree $t$,
\[
\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(E[Y\mid n](i)-Y(i)\bigr)^2\le\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(t(i)-Y(i)\bigr)^2,
\]
with excess $\sum_i(t(i)-E[Y\mid n](i))^2$. The bound is uniform in depth, size
and branching structure; moreover $\Cov(t,Y)=\Cov(t,E[Y\mid n])$, so the
near-equal-$N$ test applies verbatim to adaptive strategies.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity of band-measurability]\label{thm:adaptbad}
There exist a two-point population and a depth-$0$ tree attaining squared error
$0$ while the band mean has error $1/2$.
\end{theorem}

The hypothesis is nevertheless met by the intended strategies: any tree whose
tests are threshold comparisons $w(n(i))\ge\theta$ of free witnesses and whose
leaves are $N$-only invariants is $N$-only.

\subsection{Randomized strategies}

\begin{definition}
Given strategies $t_1,\dots,t_m$ and weights $w_j\ge0$ with $\sum_jw_j=1$, the
\emph{mixture risk} is $R=\sum_jw_j\sum_{i\in\Omega}(t_j(i)-Y(i))^2$ and the
\emph{mean predictor} is $m(i)=\sum_jw_jt_j(i)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Bias--variance identity for mixtures]\label{thm:mix}
\[
R=\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(m(i)-Y(i)\bigr)^2
+\sum_{i\in\Omega}\sum_j w_j\bigl(t_j(i)-m(i)\bigr)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}
$R$ is at least the error of the mean predictor, with equality if and only if
all strategies of positive weight agree pointwise on $\Omega$: randomization
never helps and usually strictly hurts.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Randomized barrier, with equality clause]\label{thm:rand}
If every $t_j$ is an $N$-only decision tree then
$\sum_i(E[Y\mid n](i)-Y(i))^2\le R$, uniformly in the number of strategies,
their sizes and the weights. Equality holds if and only if every strategy of
positive weight reproduces the band mean on the whole population.
\end{theorem}

\subsection{Quantization and the depth--advantage collapse}

\begin{definition}
For a finite nonempty palette $V\subset\RR$, the \emph{quantization error} of the
band means against $V$ is
$\QE(V)=\sum_{i\in\Omega}\min_{v\in V}\bigl(v-E[Y\mid n](i)\bigr)^2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Quantized barrier]\label{thm:quant}
Every $N$-only predictor with values in $V$ satisfies
\[
\QE(V)+\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(E[Y\mid n](i)-Y(i)\bigr)^2
\le\sum_{i\in\Omega}\bigl(\text{predictor}(i)-Y(i)\bigr)^2 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Depth--advantage collapse]\label{thm:depth}
Fix a palette $V$. Every $N$-only adaptive strategy whose outputs lie in $V$ ---
of any size, depth or branching pattern --- obeys the same lower bound of
Theorem~\ref{thm:quant}. Since a constant-leaf tree of size $\sigma$ emits at
most $\sigma+1$ distinct values, enlarging the tree beyond the point at which
its palette is realized cannot improve the prediction.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Strictness]\label{thm:strict}
If some band mean lies outside the palette of a strategy then $\QE(V)>0$ and the
strategy is strictly worse than the band mean.
\end{theorem}

\section{The boundary of the framework}\label{sec:boundary}

\begin{theorem}[The barriers are not information-theoretic]\label{thm:notinfo}
There is a function $g:\NN\to\NN$ of $N$ alone with $g(pq)=p$ for all primes
$p<q$: the least-prime-factor function.
\end{theorem}

Hence ``any computable function of $N$ alone is $N$-only'' must be read
\emph{structurally}. What the barriers exclude are richly structured classes:
polynomial, rational, algebraic, meromorphic, symmetric-power-sum,
multiplicative polynomial, and band-measurable statistics. Trial division
escapes them not by cleverness but by cost.

\begin{theorem}[Fine bands make the test vacuous]\label{thm:fine}
If $n$ is injective on $\Omega$ then $E[Y\mid n]=Y$ on $\Omega$, the residual
vanishes identically, and the irreducible error in Theorem~\ref{thm:sqerr} is
$0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Necessity of the constant-band-mean hypothesis]\label{thm:cex}
On the population $\Omega=\{6,15\}$ with the identity as both invariant and
target, the covariance equals $9/4>0$.
\end{theorem}

So an $N$-only invariant \emph{can} correlate with the smaller factor across
bands; every zero-correlation statement here is conditional on the band
structure.

\section{Algorithms}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Near-equal-$N$ classifier]
\emph{Input:} a population of semiprimes with known factorizations, a band width
$w$, a candidate invariant. \emph{Output:} a verdict ``$N$-only'' or
``informative'' with a certified bound. \emph{Method:} compute band labels
$\lfloor N/w\rfloor$ and band means of the target; compute
$\varepsilon=\Var(E[Y\mid n])/\Var(Y)$; report the bound $\sqrt\varepsilon$ of
Theorem~\ref{thm:bsl} together with the measured correlation. Since the bound is
uniform over all invariants, one computation per population certifies all
candidates. Complexity $O(|\Omega|\log|\Omega|)$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Generic symmetric reduction]
\emph{Input:} $F\in\ZZ[X]$, modulus $N$. \emph{Output:}
$\psi=\Psi_F(\cdot,N)\in\ZZ[s]$. \emph{Method:} run $A_{k+1}=-NB_k$,
$B_{k+1}=B_ks+A_k$ in $\ZZ[s]$ from $A_0=1,B_0=0$; accumulate
$A_F=\sum_kc_kA_k$, $B_F=\sum_kc_kB_k$; return $A_F^2+A_FB_Fs+B_F^2N$.
Complexity $O(d^2)$ coefficient operations, output degree at most $2d$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Dichotomy decision and factor recovery]
\emph{Input:} $F$, $N$, observed value $T=F(p)F(q)$. \emph{Output:} the verdict
``$N$-only'', or at most $2\deg F$ candidate factorizations. \emph{Method:}
compute $\psi$; if $\deg\psi=0$ report ``$N$-only''; else find the integer roots
$s$ of $\psi-T$ and, for each with $s^2-4N$ a perfect square, return
$\bigl((s\mp\sqrt{s^2-4N})/2\bigr)$.
\end{algorithmbox}

\begin{algorithmbox}[Polynomial success counting]
\emph{Input:} $P\in\QQ[X]$, bound $X$. \emph{Output:} the exact success set and
the certified bound $\deg P\cdot\pi(\sqrt X)$. \emph{Method:} sieve primes to
$\sqrt X$; for each $p$, find the rational roots of $P-p$ and keep those of the
form $pq$ with $q$ prime.
\end{algorithmbox}

\section{Applications and interpretation}

\paragraph{Cryptanalytic triage.} Given a claimed factoring method, ask: is the
computed object algebraic in $N$ (Sections 3, 6)? analytic in $1/N$ (Section 4)?
a symmetric function of the factor pair (Section 5.1)? does it reveal $s$
(Section 5.2), in which case it \emph{is} a factoring algorithm and its novelty
is a complexity question? does it output a difference of squares (Section 5.3)?
is it a statistic of features of $N$ (Section 7), in which case the band-spread
law bounds it? This taxonomy accounts for the overwhelming majority of
proposals.

\paragraph{Machine learning on number-theoretic features.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:agg} is blunt: any model whose inputs are computable from $N$
is a function of $N$, and its correlation with the hidden factor is bounded by
the band spread, uniformly over architectures, training procedures and dataset
sizes. The obstruction is in the input, not the model.

\paragraph{Experiment design.} Theorem~\ref{thm:fine} shows the protocol is
meaningful only for bands coarse enough to contain many semiprimes, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:bsl} quantifies the trade-off: choose the band as wide as
possible subject to $\Var(E[p\mid\text{band}])$ remaining small.

\paragraph{Complexity context.} Nothing here proves a lower bound in any
standard model of computation. Consistent with the framework, the best known
classical bound remains $L_N[1/3,1.923\ldots]$, and the only known
polynomial-time algorithm is quantum.

\section{Discussion}

\emph{The recurring mechanism.} All three rigidity barriers share one pattern:
freeze the small factor so the sample set acquires an accumulation point in the
relevant topology; invoke rigidity to collapse the function to a constant; use a
second small factor to contradict it. The pattern is robust because it uses
almost nothing about primes --- only that there are infinitely many above any
bound.

\emph{Symmetry as the true obstruction.} The modulus determines the unordered
pair $\{p,q\}$ only through its symmetric functions, and every natural invariant
one writes down is symmetric. Breaking the symmetry requires the discriminant
$s^2-4N=(q-p)^2$, whose extraction is the whole problem; Fermat's method is the
honest acknowledgement of this.

\emph{Orthogonality as a statistical no-go.} Theorem~\ref{thm:so} is elementary
--- one regrouping of a finite sum --- but its scope is unusually wide: it
applies to any predictor built from band-computable inputs, including adaptive,
randomized and quantized ones, and its quantitative form reduces an unbounded
experimental programme to a single population statistic.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.]
\item \textbf{Beyond algebraic relations.} Extend Theorem~\ref{thm:alg} from
polynomial relations to exponential polynomials in $N$ and $p$; the
freeze-and-count mechanism must be replaced, the natural tool being a
Skolem--Mahler--Lech style argument.
\item \textbf{The analytic band-spread estimate.} Everything reduces to
$\Var(E[p\mid N\text{-band}])$ for semiprimes in a size band --- a pure question
about the distribution of the smaller prime factor. A sharp estimate would make
the framework's conditional statements unconditional.
\item \textbf{Randomized strategies with oracle queries.} The finite-mixture
case is settled with its equality clause; strategies querying an oracle
adaptively during the run remain open.
\item \textbf{Sharpness of the two-way branch.} Is the bound $2\deg F$ on the
number of candidate sums attained? A finite search would settle it.
\item \textbf{Reduction-slope statistics.} The slope $B_F$ vanishes exactly when
$F(p)=F(q)$. How often, for random $F$ of given degree and random semiprimes?
\item \textbf{A counting barrier for general algebraic relations.} How many
semiprimes below $X$ can satisfy a fixed nonzero relation $F(N,p)=0$ of given
bidegree?
\item \textbf{Depth versus palette.} What is the minimal palette size needed to
approximate the band means to prescribed accuracy? This would convert the
depth--advantage collapse into an explicit size--error trade-off.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

Eight barriers stand between the natural function families and a classical
factoring formula: three rigidity theorems (polynomial/algebraic, quantitative
counting, meromorphic), two structural traps (symmetry, circularity), one
taxonomy result (known-method-in-disguise), one dichotomy valid in every degree,
and one statistical no-go --- structural orthogonality --- covering free-witness
aggregation, adaptivity, randomization and quantization at a stroke. The
framework is explicit about its limits: structural rather than
information-theoretic, conditional on genuinely coarse bands, and dependent on
the constant-band-mean hypothesis, which a two-point counterexample shows cannot
be dropped. Within those limits it explains, with proofs, the uniform
experimental failure of $284$ candidate invariants across more than sixty
paradigms.

